  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nHigh clouds have been effective in holding temperatures back a\nbit through the day, but most locations will still see\ntemperatures or heat indices in the 95-100 range. Will let the\nHeat Advisory die a natural death at 7 PM this evening. \n\nThe cold front will push through the area tonight. Minor \ndisturbances in the weak westerly flow aloft will bring multiple\nchances of showers and thunderstorms to the Quad State late\ntonight through Tuesday. \n\nThe chances begin late this evening through Monday morning \nalong the I-64 corridor. Much of this activity may remain north \nof the area, but if we do get some into the area, especially \nthis evening, there may be enough shear and instability to \nsupport a stray strong to severe storm with damaging winds. \n\nThe better chance will be late Monday afternoon over southeast\nMissouri, especially over the Ozark Foothills. Moderate\ninstability is possible over there, so a stray storm could pulse\nup close to severe levels with very isolated gusty winds. There\nis a consistent signal amongst most of the guidance to then slowly\npush scattered showers and thunderstorms eastward across the \nsouthern tip of Illinois and much of west Kentucky Monday night.\nInstability will be limited so lightning and locally heavy\nrainfall will be the primary concerns. Some showers may linger\ninto Tuesday over west Kentucky before the mid/upper-level\nenergy pushes east of the area. \n\nSurface high pressure and eventually high pressure aloft will \nthen keep the Quad State dry with seasonable temperatures\nthrough the remainder of the week and into next weekend.",
